  faqDishImage: {fileID: -2092983280216000583, guid: cd8c1c291906ae14cb0467a92c526883, type: 3}
  faqEntries:
  - type: 1
    text: "Do you know what does the word \u201Csisig\u201D actually mean? "
    answer: "Lucia \u201CAling Lucing\u201D Cunanan opened a small stall along the
      Angeles City railroad tracks in 1974. She sourced cheap pork heads from the
      nearby Clark Air Base commissaries and turned them into the dish we love today."
  - type: 1
    text: Do you know who invented modern pork sisig?
    answer: "In Kapampangan, \u201Csisig\u201D originally referred to anything prepared
      with sour ingredients. Diego Berga\xF1o\u2019s 1732 Kapampangan dictionary
      recorded it as a snack of unripe guava or papaya with vinegar and salt, often
      eaten by pregnant women. "
  - type: 1
    text: 'Do you know why is the chopping done on a wooden board rather than in
      a blender? '
    answer: The three-stage boil-grill-chop method produces a controlled contrast
      between the chewy outer skin and the softer interior. Over-mincing in a blender
      destroys this diagnostic texture, which is why heritage preparations keep the
      cleaver and the wooden board
  - type: 1
    text: ' Do you know if Clark Air Base was really the source of the pork?'
    answer: 'The base commissaries from 1947 to 1991 were the economic condition
      that made cheap pork head and ears available in volume to local Angeles City
      vendors. The peer-reviewed heritage literature documents this as the material
      foundation of modern sisig. '
